        <p class="profile-detail-region">Culture · Europe</p>
        <h1 class="profile-detail-name">Cucuteni–Trypillia culture</h1>
        <div class="info-stats" style="margin-top:10px;">
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Homeland</span><span class="info-stat-val">From the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carpathian-mountains">Carpathian Mountains</a> to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dniester">Dniester</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dnieper">Dnieper</a>, about 350,000 km², centred on present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moldova">Moldova</a> with large parts of western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ukraine">Ukraine</a> and north-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romania">Romania</a>; its historical core on the middle to upper Dniester; key sites Cucuteni, Trypillia, Talianki, Poduri, Verteba Cave, Poiana Slatinei</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Period</span><span class="info-stat-val">c. 5050–2950 BCE in all, in Early, Middle and Late periods; the Romanian scheme (Hubert Schmidt, 1932) runs Pre-Cucuteni I–III, Cucuteni A and B, then Horodiștea–Foltești, the Ukrainian (Tatiana Passek, 1949) Trypillia A, B and C; mega-sites in the middle phase, c. 4100–3500 BCE</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Known for</span><span class="info-stat-val">painted pottery with spiral designs, later fired in two-chamber kilns; clay figurines, many of them female; mega-sites of up to three thousand structures; settlements burned and rebuilt every 60–80 years; salt from springs, as at Poiana Slatinei; miniature wheeled models; the “Cucuteni Frumusica Dance”</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">How we know them</span><span class="info-stat-val">Excavations at Cucuteni from 1885 (Teodor T. Burada and scholars from Iași) and at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyiv">Kyiv</a> and Trypillia from the 1890s (Vincenc Chvojka); about 3,000 sites known by 2003; geophysical surveys and the Collaborative Research Centre 1266 at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kiel">Kiel</a> University; isotope studies; ancient DNA from Verteba Cave and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moldova">Moldova</a> (Nikitin et al. 2010 and 2017, Mathieson et al. 2018, Immel et al. 2020); no writing</span></div>
        </div>
