    <section class="kid-tale" aria-labelledby="kid-tale-title">
      <h2 class="kid-tale-title" id="kid-tale-title">The story of the Cucuteni–Trypillia culture</h2>
      <ol class="kid-tale-pages"><li><figure><img src="/images/encyclopedia/prut.jpg" alt="Prut" loading="lazy" width="320" height="220"><figcaption>Prut <span class="kid-tale-cr">Varga Attila, CC BY-SA 3.0</span></figcaption></figure><p>About 7,000 years ago, a farming people began to spread out from the land around the Prut river.</p><p>They settled from the Carpathian Mountains to the Dnieper river, in today’s Moldova, Ukraine and Romania.</p></li><li><figure><img src="/images/encyclopedia/pulses.jpg" alt="Pulses" loading="lazy" width="320" height="220"><figcaption>Pulses <span class="kid-tale-cr">Keith Weller, Agricultural Research Service, USDA, Public domain</span></figcaption></figure><p>They grew wheat, barley, rye, millet, peas and beans, and kept cattle, pigs, sheep and goats.</p></li><li class="kid-tale-np"><p>Some of their villages grew huge, with up to three thousand buildings, among the biggest in Europe and Asia.</p><p>Every 60 to 80 years people burned their villages and built them again, and nobody knows for sure why.</p></li><li><figure><img src="/images/encyclopedia/copper.jpg" alt="Copper" loading="lazy" width="320" height="220"><figcaption>Copper <span class="kid-tale-cr">Jonathan Zander; derivative work by Materialscientist, CC BY-SA 3.0</span></figcaption></figure><p>Potters painted swirling spirals in black, red and yellow, and people made tools of stone, bone and later copper.</p><p>They also made little clay animals and cups on four wheels, which may have been toys.</p></li><li><figure><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kurgan.jpg" alt="Kurgan" loading="lazy" width="320" height="220"><figcaption>Kurgan <span class="kid-tale-cr">Public domain</span></figcaption></figure><p>Late in their history, herders from the steppe, the grasslands to the east, came to live beside them.</p><p>Their way of life ended almost 5,000 years ago, and experts still argue about why.</p></li><li><figure><img src="/images/encyclopedia/southern-bug.jpg" alt="Southern Bug" loading="lazy" width="320" height="220"><figcaption>Southern Bug <span class="kid-tale-cr">George Chernilevsky, CC BY-SA 3.0</span></figcaption></figure><p>Archaeologists, people who dig up the past, have found about 3,000 of their sites.</p><p>Their painted pots and clay figures are kept in museums in Ukraine, Romania and Russia.</p></li></ol>
    </section>

    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>The culture spread from the eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carpathian-mountains">Carpathians</a> to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dnieper">Dnieper</a>, and in its middle phase its people built some of the largest settlements in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eurasia">Eurasia</a>, a few with as many as three thousand structures.</li><li>Its settlements were burned and rebuilt every 60 to 80 years, sometimes on the plan of the old buildings, for reasons scholars still debate.</li><li>How it ended is disputed: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marija-gimbutas">Marija Gimbutas</a> saw invasion from the steppe, others gradual change or a drier climate, and ancient DNA shows steppe-related ancestry entering late in its history.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>At Poduri in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romania">Romania</a> thirteen habitation levels were built one on top of another over many years, as the settlement was destroyed and rebuilt on the same spot.</p></aside>
    </section>

